# Commits that rewrote many files without changing their *content* — line endings, encoding,
# whitespace, file shape. Listing one here keeps `git blame` pointing at whoever last wrote the line
# rather than at the sweep that reflowed it.
#
# The bar is content, not behaviour. A commit can leave behaviour identical and still be the true
# author of the lines it added, and ignoring that one hides real authorship. Stated precisely: a
# commit belongs here only if it *wrote no code* — every surviving line is either byte-identical to
# its parent once whitespace and the BOM are discarded, or a formatter's re-spelling of a
# declaration that was already there and still says the same thing. Deleting a line is also fine,
# since a deleted line has no blame to misattribute. Every entry below was verified that way, file
# by file, before being added.
#
# The first two entries meet the stricter "nothing but whitespace" reading. The third does not, and
# that is why the wording above is what it is: converting `namespace X { … }` to `namespace X;`
# replaces the declaration line outright, and the sweep also deletes redundant `using` lines. Both
# are a formatter rewriting a declaration nobody re-authored, which is exactly the case this file
# exists for — but neither is whitespace, so do not let the looser bar in and then apply it to a
# commit that genuinely wrote something.
#
# The fourth entry stretches it once more, and this is as far as it goes. A primary-constructor
# conversion re-spells the *references* too — `_context` becomes `context` on every line that used
# it — so lines deep inside method bodies change, which none of the first three do. It qualifies on
# one condition, and only this condition: the rename is total and mechanical. Every occurrence of
# one identifier within one class is rewritten, the identifier still denotes the same object, and
# nothing else on the line moves. A commit that renames some occurrences, or renames a thing into a
# different thing, has written code and does not belong here no matter how mechanical it looked.
# The dependency removals that sweep uncovered are a separate commit for exactly that reason.
#
# This is also why the encoding work is three commits rather than one: the commit that *wrote*
# .gitattributes and .editorconfig is deliberately kept out of the two sweeps, so ignoring the
# sweeps does not also hide the authorship of the policy itself.
#
# Enable locally with:
#   git config blame.ignoreRevsFile .git-blame-ignore-revs
#
# GitHub reads this file automatically, so blame in the web UI already skips these.

